on's code, and the view cycle, settings, pause menu and saves came for free.\",\n    \"Yes. map.center returns an array, not {x, y}; E.tex.flagstone needs a palette; Body already has a step field, so a method named step breaks it. I guessed each one from a stack trace.\",\n    \"The conversation runner: Dialog's page and choice flow (choices appear only on the last page, and confirm on the last page closes the box and calls onChoice and then onDone). The engine's helpers covered rendering, not the story flow.\",\n    \"The Foe class and its step/attack loop, the world's chapters, the attract AI, the hub ghosts and the About pages. The engine supplies the parts; the game's rules are mine.\",\n    \"Hooking the hub: the connect promise resolves on a later turn, and the headless tests needed an explicit await. Also the fake hub client that the tests had to write, since the game only sees the real protocol through the harness.\",\n    \"I'd remove the undocumented Body.step counter (or rename it to something that cannot collide with a game's own method). Its name is the most common verb in game code, and the failure looks like a broken world rather than a naming clash.\",\n    \"Make the tex helpers and map.center state their defaults and return shapes in the API card, and let flagstone take a default palette. Both cost real time.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"The concrete done-list, with the harness and link to the hub's protocol. It gave a checklist I could test against, and the exit interview shape left no guesswork.\",\n    \"The overlap of 'Do not read files outside what you need' with the need to read the engine's docs (about 300 KB of them). The two Done conditions also ask for a file at the root and the html in the folder: I wrote both where they are named, but the root-or-subfolder split took a re-read.\",\n    \"The full playthrough and automated tests for every system within one session. The scope is large for a single pass, so most of the effort went into the test harness, and the visual polish came last.\",\n    \"A browser test that plays the whole game with the real keyboard and mouse, with a draw pass in each chapter, and screenshots compared against a reference. That would have caught the draw crash before the Chromium run.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"SOPRANOLAND\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Sunday gravy is on the table.\",\n    \"description\": \"Run the town of Bellmore Point for Ma: collect from the shops, sit down with Big Lou, see Dr. Vashti, take the Pappalardo yard from Gus and get home before the Feds do.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Action\",\n    \"source\": \"The Sopranos (1999-2007)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Move: left stick or WASD. Bat: X or J or left click. Pistol: LT or Q or middle click. Guard: LB or K or right click. Roll: RB or L. Talk: Y or E. Jump: A or Space. Camera: R3 or V\",\n    \"tags\": [\"third-person\", \"comedy\", \"boss fights\", \"exploration\", \"story\"]\n  }\n}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01J7NJRoR4jRGMDgyXDjWyj7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
